all\n- Pre-flop with A♣K♥ - a premium starting hand (top 2% of hands)\n\n**Hand Strength:**\nA-K offsuit is one of the strongest starting hands in Texas Hold'em. It dominates many hands and has excellent equity against most ranges.\n\n**Strategic Considerations:**\n\n1. **Check (0):** Too passive with such a strong hand. Gives free card and doesn't build pot.\n\n2. **Fold (-1):** Absurd with AK. This is a premium hand.\n\n3. **Raise (200+):** Standard play with AK to:\n   - Build the pot with a strong hand\n   - Gain information about opponent's hand strength\n   - Take initiative in the hand\n   - Potentially win immediately if opponent folds\n\n4. **All-in (-2):** Very aggressive but could be justified because:\n   - It's the penultimate hand - chip pressure matters\n   - AK has strong equity against most hands\n   - Forces opponent to make a tough decision\n   - If I win, I'd have ~40,000 chips going into the final hand with huge advantage\n   - Risk: Opponent only calls with premium hands (QQ+, AK), making it closer to a coinflip\n\n**Optimal Strategy:**\nA standard raise to 200-600 chips (3-6x big blind assuming standard blinds) is the most balanced approach. It builds the pot while maintaining flexibility to fold if facing heavy resistance.\n\nHowever, given:\n- Second-to-last hand creates urgency\n- Chip stacks are nearly even (difference of only 100 chips)\n- AK is strong enough to get value from weaker holdings\n- A big raise or all-in puts maximum pressure\n\nReason:\nI'll raise to 800 chips - a substantial raise that builds the pot significantly with my premium AK hand while not fully committing. This puts pressure on my opponent, builds value with a strong holding, and maintains some post-flop playability. It's aggressive enough to capitalize on my hand strength while being more balanced than going all-in pre-flop.\n\nAnswer: 800"}
